      "abstract": "reason. Chapter 2 discusses the theological genesis and subsequent secularization of science, re-examining misinterpretations of Aquinas to argue for an authentic Thomist theology of human participation in the divine. Chapter 3 focuses on Jacques Derrida, whose writings yield potential for a theological critique of the modern, secular problem of the separation of faith and reason; Deagon welcomes that Derrida\u2019s words are replete with theological references (p. 71) while recognizing the strong pagan dimension to his ontology (p. 73). Chapter 4 uses Derrida, Walter Benjamin et al. to expose the inseparability of human law and violence, while Chapter 5 \u2018continues the process of recalibrating jurisprudence in the shape of a Christian ontology of peace\u2019 (p. 13). Chapter 6 offers a re-reading of Paul\u2019s law of love, influenced by Slavoj \u017di\u017eek, in order to articulate a genuine Christian materialism. The alternative to a secular philosophy that sunders faith and reason is developed \u2018in the form of the Incarnational Paradox\u2019 (p. 161). Chapter 7 returns to Augustine to explore the idea of a peaceful legal community that emerges from the incarnation. The distinctive, powerful theological arguments in this book are likely to polarize opinion. Few readers in Christian tradition are likely to dispute the idea at its heart: namely, that \u2018[r]eturning reason to its ground in being allows peaceful persuasion by the revelation of God\u2019s perfect being through the Trinity and Incarnation, which models and enables the peaceful coexistence of difference through self-sacrificing love\u2019 (p. 1). More might find it difficult to stomach the unqualified way in which he presents the Church as a contrast community, and Christian faith as an alternative (or the true) jurisprudence. I find this book too salty \u2013 but perhaps this is as much a matter of personal taste in theological expression than substantive difference. Instead, I opt for a theology of the resurrection that both judges and affirms all creaturely existence assumed by Christ Jesus at the incarnation. While appreciative of the experience, I prefer a theological approach that attends more constructively to the nature and complexity of service for those disciples of Christ who strive to live out their witness in secular legal contexts.",
